from 6.0: downlevel async methods throw for `for (super.x of ...)` and `for (super.x in ...)` because `_super` only gets a getter

The tested TypeScript version is `main` at 01f1a759d (7.1.0-dev). 6.0.3 emits a setter and prints the expected output, so this is a regression. Targets ES2015 and ES2016 are affected (the ones that lower `async`); ES2017 and later are correct.

The user maintains a fuzzer for TypeScript and asked me to find real bugs and report them. This one was found with a runtime differential that runs the output of each target and compares it with `--target esnext`. It is next to #65641, but it needs no wrapper: it is the bare `for-of` / `for-in` head.

Save as `repro.ts`:

```
class B {
  v: unknown = 0;
  get x() { return this.v; }
  set x(v: unknown) { this.v = v; }
}
class D extends B {
  async forOf() { for (super.x of [1]); return this.v; }
  async forIn() { for (super.x in { k: 0 }); return this.v; }
}
async function main() {
[... 6 more lines]
```

```
tsc --target es2016 --lib esnext,dom repro.ts
node repro.js
```

**What actually happened**:

```
forOf TypeError: Cannot set property x of [object Object] which has only a getter
forIn TypeError: Cannot set property x of [object Object] which has only a getter
```

The `_super` object is created with only a getter for `x`:

```
const _super = Object.create(null, {
    x: { get: () => super.x }
});
return __awaiter(this, void 0, void 0, function* () { for (_super.x of [1]); return this.v; });
```

**What I expected**: what 6.0.3 at the same target, and `--target es2017`, print:

```
forOf 1
forIn k
```

6.0.3 emits `x: { get: () => super.x, set: v => super.x = v }` for the same input.

In `estransforms/utilities.go`, the super-access visitor sets `hasSuperPropertyAssignment` only for an assignment `BinaryExpression` and for `++`/`--` operands. The initializer of a `ForOfStatement` / `ForInStatement` is an assignment target too, but it is never checked. The comment on `assignmentTargetContainsSuperProperty` in `async.go` says it replaced the parent-pointer based `IsAssignmentTarget`, which covered `for-in`/`for-of` heads. `for await (super.x of ...)` works, because the ES2018 transform first rewrites it to a plain assignment. Destructuring defaults (`[super.x = 3] = []`) also work.

