Downlevel async methods lose `this` for `super.m` tagged templates and for wrapped `super.m()` calls, and turn wrapped `super.x = v` into a getter-only write

The tested TypeScript version is `main` at 01f1a759d (7.1.0-dev). 6.0.3 emits the same, so this is not a regression. Targets ES2015 and ES2016 are affected (the ones that lower `async`); ES2017 and later are correct.

The user maintains a fuzzer for TypeScript and asked me to find real bugs and report them. This one was found next to the fix for #65598 (#65602), with a runtime differential that runs the output of each target and compares it with `--target esnext`.

Save as `repro.ts`:

```
class B {
  v = 0;
  get x() { return this.v; }
  set x(v: number) { this.v = v; }
  m(..._: unknown[]) { return this?.constructor?.name; }
}
class D extends B {
  async run() {
    console.log(super.m(), (super.m)(), (super.m as any)(), super.m!());
    console.log(super.m`t`, (super.m)`t`);
[... 5 more lines]
```

```
tsc --target es2016 repro.ts
node repro.js
```

**What actually happened**:

```
D undefined undefined undefined
undefined undefined
TypeError: Cannot set property x of [object Object] which has only a getter
```

The async body is emitted as:

```
const _super = Object.create(null, {
    m: { get: () => super.m },
    x: { get: () => super.x }
});
return __awaiter(this, void 0, void 0, function* () {
    console.log(_super.m.call(this), (_super.m)(), _super.m(), _super.m());
    console.log(_super.m `t`, (_super.m) `t`);
    _super.x = 1;
    console.log(this.v);
});
```

Three problems:

1. Only a bare `super.m(...)` gets `.call(this, ...)`. Once the callee is parenthesized or has `as`, `!` or `satisfies`, it is called with `this` set to the null-prototype `_super` object.
2. A `super.m` tag never gets a receiver, even without any wrapper: ``super.m`t` `` becomes ``_super.m `t` ``.
3. `(super.x as any) = 1` is not detected as a `super` assignment, so `_super` only gets a getter for `x` and the write throws. A plain `(super.x) = 1` is detected.

**What I expected**: what `--target es2017` prints:

```
D D D D
D D
1
```

In `estransforms/utilities.go`, `substituteCallExpressionWithSuperAccess` checks `call.Expression` without skipping outer expressions, and the super-access visitor has no case for `TaggedTemplateExpression`. In `async.go`, `assignmentTargetContainsSuperProperty` skips `ParenthesizedExpression` but not `AsExpression`, `SatisfiesExpression`, `NonNullExpression` or type assertions.

