Declaration emit drops nested destructured `require` bindings in JS (`const { b: { c } } = require("./m")`)

The tested TypeScript version is 7.0.2 (also `main` at 01f1a759d). 6.0.3 is correct.

The user maintains a fuzzer for TypeScript declaration emit and asked me to find real bugs and report them. This one was found by probing the code changed by #65498.

```
// m.js
module.exports = { b: { c: "s" } };
```

```
// lib.js
const { b: { c } } = require("./m");
module.exports.c = c;
```

```
// use.ts
import lib = require("./out/lib");
const s: string = lib.c;
```

Run `tsc --allowJs --checkJs --declaration --emitDeclarationOnly --strict --module commonjs --outDir out lib.js`, then check `use.ts`.

**What actually happened**: 7.x drops the nested binding and emits an empty import:

```
export { c };
import {} from "./m";
```

`c` is never declared, so `use.ts` fails with `out/lib.d.ts(1,10): error TS2304: Cannot find name 'c'`. The same happens with an array pattern (`const { arr: [first] } = require("./m")`) and when the pattern also has a plain binding (`const { a, b: { c } } = require("./m")` emits `import { a } from "./m"` and still no `c`).

**What I expected**: what 6.0.3 emits:

```
export const c: string;
```

In `transformCjsRequireVariableDeclaration` (`internal/transformers/declarations/transform.go`), elements whose name is not an identifier are skipped (`continue // nested destructuring, bail`). Since #65498, nested bindings are declared as variables only when the same pattern also has a dynamic key, because `canTransformCjsRequireToImport` only looks for dynamic property names.

Claude Code + Claude Fable 5 + Skill Version 1.0

Declaration emit omits `resolution-mode` when an import type names a `.d.cts` file of a dual package from an ES module

The tested TypeScript version is 7.0.2 (also `main` at 01f1a759d). 6.0.3 is correct.

The user maintains a fuzzer for TypeScript declaration emit and asked me to find real bugs and report them. This one was found by probing module specifier generation for packages with conditional `exports`.

```
// package.json
{ "type": "module" }
```

```
// node_modules/pkg/package.json
{
  "name": "pkg",
  "exports": {
    ".": { "import": "./dist/index.mjs", "require": "./dist/index.cjs" },
    "./thing": { "import": "./dist/thing.mjs", "require": "./dist/thing.cjs" }
  }
}
```

```
// node_modules/pkg/dist/index.d.mts
import { Thing } from "./thing.cjs";
export declare function make(): Thing;
```

```
// node_modules/pkg/dist/thing.d.cts
export interface Thing { cjs: number }
```

```
// node_modules/pkg/dist/thing.d.mts
export interface Thing { esm: string }
```

```
// src/index.ts
import { make } from "pkg";
export const v = make();
```

```
// consumer.ts
import { v } from "./out/src/index.js";
export const n: number = v.cjs;
```

Run `tsc --declaration --emitDeclarationOnly --strict --module nodenext --rootDir . --outDir out src/index.ts`, then check `consumer.ts` with `--module nodenext`.

**What actually happened**: 7.x names the CommonJS file through the `require` condition but writes the import type without a resolution mode:

```
export declare const v: import("pkg/thing").Thing;
```

From an ES module, `pkg/thing` resolves through the `import` condition to `thing.d.mts`, so `v` silently becomes a different type: `consumer.ts` fails with `TS2339: Property 'cjs' does not exist on type 'Thing'`. There is no error when the declaration is emitted. If `./thing` only has a `require` condition, the emitted `.d.ts` fails with `TS2307: Cannot find module 'pkg/thing'`. The same happens with `node16`, with nested `types` conditions, and in array, return type and class property positions. With `--moduleResolution bundler`, 6.0.3 reports TS2883 and 7.x emits the same wrong `import("pkg/thing")`.

**What I expected**: what 6.0.3 emits:

```
export declare const v: import("pkg/thing", { with: { "resolution-mode": "require" } }).Thing;
```

In `tryDirectoryWithPackageJson` (`internal/modulespecifiers/specifiers.go`), the `exports` conditions come from the target file's extension (`.cjs`/`.cts`/`.d.cts` use `require`) instead of the importing file's mode. The first call from `getSpecifierForModuleSymbol` therefore succeeds, and the mode-swap retry in `nodebuilderimpl.go`, which is what sets `resolution-mode`, never runs. A `.d.ts` target in a CommonJS package still gets the attribute, because its extension does not force the mode.

