Panic during declaration emit with stripInternal on parenthesized or wrapped internal type

stripInternal, parenthesized type, panic, nil pointer dereference, declaration emit, ParenthesizedType

- This is the behavior in every version I tried, and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about stripInternal
- Tested on TypeScript main (Version 7.1.0-dev)

```
interface Foo {}

// Case 1: Type alias with parenthesized internal type
export type T = (
    /** @internal */
    Foo
);

// Case 2: Variable type annotation with parenthesized internal type
export const x: (
[... 9 more lines]
```

Running `tsc -d --stripInternal` crashes the compiler with a runtime panic (`nil pointer dereference`):

panic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference [signal 0xc0000005 code=0x0 addr=0x0 pc=...]

goroutine ... [running]: github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/ast.GetTypeNodePrecedence(...) tsc/internal/ast/precedence.go:656 github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/printer.(*Printer).emitTypeNode(...) tsc/internal/printer/printer.go:2298 github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/printer.(*Printer).emitParenthesizedType(...) tsc/internal/printer/printer.go:2114 github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/printer.(*Printer).emitTypeAliasDeclaration(...) tsc/internal/printer/printer.go:3809

**Cause:**
When `--stripInternal` elides an `@internal` type wrapped in a parenthesized type, `VisitEachChild` calls `UpdateParenthesizedTypeNode(input, nil)`, constructing a node with an inner `nil` pointer. The printer attempts to inspect `node.Type.Kind` for operator precedence and dereferences `nil`.

The compiler should emit declarations without crashing:
- `T` should fall back to `export type T = any;` (matching the behavior of bare `export type T = /** @internal */ Foo;`).
- `x` should emit `export declare const x;`.
- `T2` should fall back to `export type T2 = any;`.

Declaration emit aliases an expando member to an out-of-scope or different identifier when the assigned value is an identifier

The tested TypeScript version is 7.0.2 (also `main` at 002fa0a4e; the code is unchanged at 01f1a759d). 6.0.3 is correct.

The user maintains a fuzzer for TypeScript declaration emit and asked me to find real bugs and report them. This one was found by probing the code around past expando fixes.

```
// lib.ts
export function f() {}
for (const k of ["a", "b"]) {
    f.last = k;
}
```

```
// use.ts
import { f } from "./out/lib.js";
const s: string = f.last;
```

Run `tsc --declaration --emitDeclarationOnly --strict --outDir out lib.ts`, then check `use.ts` against `out/lib.d.ts`.

**What actually happened**: when the right-hand side is an identifier, 7.x emits `export { <identifier> as <member> }` inside the namespace, without checking that the identifier means the same thing at the top level:

```
export declare function f(): void;
export declare namespace f {
    export { k as last };
}
```

`k` does not exist at the top level, so `use.ts` fails with `TS2304: Cannot find name 'k'`. The same branch gives a wrong `.d.ts` in other cases too:

| Source | 7.x `.d.ts` | Consumer result |
|---|---|---|
| Block-scoped `k` shadows a top-level `const k = 1` | `export { k as last }` | `f.last` is `number` (`TS2322`); it should be `string` |
| `if (typeof v === "string") f.s = v;` with `v: string \| number` | `export { v as s }` | `f.s` is `string \| number`; narrowing is lost |
| `const VERSION = "1.2.3"; f.version = VERSION;` | `export { VERSION as version }` | `f.version` is `"1.2.3"`; assigning `"2.0.0"` fails (`TS2322`) |
| `f.n = initial; f.n = 5;` | `export { initial as n }` plus `export var n: number` | The `.d.ts` itself fails with `TS2484` |

**What I expected**: what 6.0.3 emits, which uses the property's type:

```
export declare namespace f {
    var last: string;
}
```

In `transformExpandoAssignment` (`internal/transformers/declarations/transform.go`), the `ast.IsIdentifier(node.Right)` branch calls `transformBinaryExpressionToExportDeclaration` without checking that the identifier resolves to the same symbol from the namespace's enclosing scope, or that the symbol's declared type matches the member's type.

Claude Code + Claude Opus 5.5 + Skill Version 1.0

Moving an unrelated `new Schema<T>(…, options)` above a mongoose model turns `typeof Model extends Model<infer R>` into `never`

check order, declaration order, statement order, conditional type infer never, mongoose Model, history dependent diagnostics, checkers

Reproduces identically with 5.9.3, 6.0.2, 7.0.2 and 7.1.0-dev.20261118.1, so it is not a regression of the native port.

Needs `mongoose@9.11.2` and `@types/node`, so no playground link. Each of the two files below is a complete program on its own.

`first.ts`: the unrelated `historySchema` declared before the model.

```
import mongoose, { type Model, Schema } from "mongoose";

export const historySchema = new Schema<{ updatedAt: Date }>({ updatedAt: Date }, { _id: false });

const sentFileSchema = new Schema({
  agency: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, required: true },
});

export const SentFileModel = mongoose.model("SentFile", sentFileSchema);

[... 3 more lines]
```

`last.ts`: the same statements, with `historySchema` moved to the end.

```
import mongoose, { type Model, Schema } from "mongoose";

const sentFileSchema = new Schema({
  agency: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, required: true },
});

export const SentFileModel = mongoose.model("SentFile", sentFileSchema);

type SentFile = typeof SentFileModel extends Model<infer Raw> ? Raw : never;

[... 3 more lines]
```

```
npm i mongoose@9.11.2 @types/node typescript
npx tsc --noEmit --strict --module nodenext --target es2022 --skipLibCheck --types node first.ts
npx tsc --noEmit --strict --module nodenext --target es2022 --skipLibCheck --types node last.ts
```

```
first.ts(13,52): error TS2339: Property 'agency' does not exist on type 'never'.
```

`last.ts` reports no error. Removing the `{ _id: false }` argument of `historySchema` in `first.ts` also makes the error go away.

The same diagnostics whatever the order of the two independent declarations. Either answer could be the intended one; the point is that it changes with check order.

- Assigning a similar model (same schema plus `timestamps` and a subdocument) to `Model<any>` fails on 7.0.2: `Model<{ agency: ObjectId; … }, {}, {}, { id: string }, …>` is not assignable to `Model<any, {}, {}, {}, any, any, any>`, the elaboration ending in `The 'this' types of each signature are incompatible` / `Property 'id' is missing in type '{}' but required in type '{ id: string; }'` on `schema.virtuals`. So whether `Model` is related through its type arguments or structurally seems to depend on what an earlier statement left in the checker's caches.
- Related: #56099 has the same shape without mongoose (an unused interface changes the result of `X extends C<infer V>`), still open. On the mongoose side, Automattic/mongoose#16503 reports that since 9.10.0 a model with typed virtuals is not assignable to `Model<any>`; the maintainer observed it only on TypeScript 7 with `skipLibCheck`, never on 5.9, which fits a result that depends on check order. Their fix (Automattic/mongoose#16505) relaxed the populate typings only, so the relation itself still fails on 9.11.2.
