Object-rest for-of lowering skips transformations on direct body statements

AI assistance: I used OpenAI Codex to investigate this behavior and prepare this report and proposed regression test.

`"object rest" "nested"`, `"object rest" "for-of"`, `"object rest" "function declaration"`, `"objectRestSpreadTransformer"`, `"visitForOftatement"`, `"object rest" "ReferenceError"`. Both issues and PRs were searched across states.

Reproduced on macOS arm64 with Node 24.22.2 using TypeScript 7.0.2, nightly 7.1.0-dev.20261115.1, and a native source build at `6bdcb54d23f41bc7098c1f3488ac599c75b852ca` (`Version 7.1.0-dev`). The isolated inputs also work with TypeScript 6.0.3.

Save as `repro.ts`:

```
let sum = 0;
for (const {...outer} of [{a: 1}]) {
    for (const {...inner} of [{b: 2}]) {
        sum += outer.a + inner.b;
    }
}
console.log(sum);
```

Run:

```
tsc repro.ts --target es2015 --module commonjs --ignoreConfig --outDir out
node out/repro.js
```

Expected: compilation succeeds and the emitted JavaScript prints `3`.

Actual: compilation exits successfully without diagnostics, but the inner loop is emitted as:

```
for (const inner = __rest(void 0, []) of [{ b: 2 }]) {
    sum += outer.a + inner.b;
}
```

Node rejects this with `SyntaxError: for-of loop variable declaration may not have an initializer.` The unbraced nested-loop form has the same failure.

A direct function declaration in the outer loop body shows the same skipped root transformation:

```
let result = 0;
for (const {...outer} of [{a: 1}]) {
    function read({value, ...rest}: {value: number, factor: number}) {
        return value + rest.factor;
    }
    result = outer.a + read({value: 2, factor: 3});
}
console.log(result);
```

Expected: `6`. Actual: the emitted function has a generated parameter name but no destructuring prologue, so execution throws `ReferenceError: value is not defined`.

ES2015 and ES2017 reproduce both failures. ES2018 and ESNext pass. Single-rest-loop, extra-block, and function-expression controls pass. The proposed regression also checks calls before the function declaration, per-iteration closures, and `continue` behavior.

In [`visitForOftatement`](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/6bdcb54d23f41bc7098c1f3488ac599c75b852ca/tsc/internal/transformers/estransforms/objectrestspread.go#L459), both the block and nonblock branches visit each body's children without dispatching its root. A nested for-of statement therefore misses its own lowering, and a function declaration misses its parameter/body transformation.

Valid source silently produces unloadable or broken output for targets below ES2018; tested source/target workarounds exist, and no persisted data loss was demonstrated.

Bounded all-state issue and PR searches on 2026-11-15 located related [#23122](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/pull/23122)/[#23124](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/pull/23124) (omitted nonblock bodies), [#43400](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/43400)/[#52922](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/pull/52922) (nested assignment patterns), and [#65493](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/pull/65493) (malformed bindings and printer panic). No exact duplicate was located; this is not an exhaustive novelty claim.

Local verification: the original transformer fails the new ES2015/ES2017 compiler output baselines while ES2018 passes. Replacing the two body calls with `VisitNode` passes all three configurations and all 28 isolated runtime checks. Build and 60 codegen tests pass. `npx hereby validate --all` passed: compiler/language-service Go (170,463 tests reported, 2,145 skipped), extension (57 passed), native API (1,077 passed), WASM API (405 + 564 passed, two skipped), tools/options, declaration/Herebyfile checks, compiler smoke, lint and formatting. Tidy passed without manifest/module changes. Benchmarks, browser API smoke, and the full `test:wasm` suite were not run.

A local fix and regression test are prepared. Following the PR template, I will wait for this issue to reach the Backlog milestone before opening the PR.

