API client: concurrent `Program.getSourceFile` calls for one file stampede the cache, each fetching the file whole

In the JS API client (`typescript/async`), `Program.getSourceFile` checks the source file cache and, on a miss, sends
`getSourceFile` to the server. The result is cached only once its response is decoded. N calls for the same file that
start before the first response arrives each miss the cache (a cache stampede), so the server sends and the client
decodes the whole file N times. The cache's `set` then returns the existing object, so callers all get one `SourceFile`
and the work of the other N−1 fetches is thrown away.

The same `Program` already avoids this for its other per-file lookup: `getSourceFileMetadata` keeps the request's
promise in `sourceFileMetadataCache`, so concurrent calls for one file share one `getSourceFileMetadata` request.
`getSourceFile` has no such sharing.

The code is `Program.getSourceFile` in `_packages/native-preview/src/api/async/api.ts` (main, 2026-11-14; shipped as
`getSourceFileWorker` in `dist/api/async/api.js` of `typescript@7.1.0-dev.20261114.1`):

```
