False TS7027 and wrong narrowing when the left operand of `??` is falsy but not nullish

nullish coalescing, ??, ??=, TS7027, Unreachable code detected, control flow, narrowing, falsy left operand, bindLogicalLikeExpression

- This is the behavior in every version I tried: `typescript@7.1.0-dev.20261108.1`, `7.0.2`, `6.0.3` and `5.9.3`, with `tsc --noEmit --strict --allowUnreachableCode false --target esnext` on linux-x64.

[Playground link](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?allowUnreachableCode=false#code/CYUwxgNghgTiAEAzArgOzAFwJYHtXzAAoBKALngCMccIQpUBuAKCYHcALLW+Qo4+APwCefeAB8x8DDGQhi-AN4BfJn2ZMU6bHiSEo5AEQHx8NKERZUIYABpK5KjTqpFTePCyIeUQcIqKleBAIAGcEBTd3AjwQjFNyMxALK2B4AF54KGZ3FRUgA)

```
declare function c(): boolean;

while (c() ?? (c() || true)) {}
c();

function f(a: "" | undefined, b: boolean) {
  if (a ?? b) {} else {
    const u: undefined = a;
  }
}
```

The call after the loop is reported unreachable, and the assignment in the else branch has no error:

```
issue.ts(4,1): error TS7027: Unreachable code detected.
```

No TS7027 on line 4: when the first `c()` returns `false`, `??` returns `false` without evaluating its right operand, so the loop exits. Line 8 reports TS2322, because the else branch also runs when `a` is `""`.

[`bindLogicalLikeExpression`](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/f7973164a5f079a2d8b88d0fdb39347222549124/tsc/internal/binder/binder.go#L2299-L2317) binds the left operand of `??` and `??=` as it binds the left of `||`, and the checker reads that condition as "not nullish". A left operand that is not nullish but falsy, such as `false`, `0` or `""`, makes the whole expression falsy without evaluating the right operand, yet the flow graph has no path from the left operand to the false branch. The false branch is then reached only through the right operand, so it is unreachable when the right operand cannot be false, and otherwise it narrows as if the left operand were nullish.

The fix I propose adds that path when the left operand can be falsy and non-nullish. The binder does not use types, so a typed left operand that can never be falsy and non-nullish, such as `a: object`, also gets the path and loses its TS7027, as `while (a)` with `a: true` already gives none. The else branch then narrows as `if (!a)` does, so `if (a ?? b) {} else { const r: false = b; }` with `a: object | undefined` gets TS2322, as `if (a && b)` with `a: object` already does.

I met this while building deadset-ts, the TypeScript analyzer of [deadset](https://github.com/cplieger/deadset). A pull request with the fix and a test follows.

