`export *` resolution allocates per re-exported name, slowing checks of barrels over large modules

export star, export *, barrel file, re-export performance, getExportsOfModule, extendExportSymbols, slow check

- Observed on `main` (7.1.0-dev, aa8149273b, 2026-10-09). I have not bisected; the per-name tracking described below has been part of the export resolution code for a long time.

Not reproducible in the playground; it needs a generated project. Generator below.

`node gen.mjs && tsgo -p . --extendedDiagnostics`

```
// One module with many exports, re-exported through a chain of `export *` barrels and imported by many files.
import fs from "node:fs";
const EXPORTS = 20000, DEPTH = 6, USERS = 200;
fs.mkdirSync("src", { recursive: true });
let big = "";
for (let i = 0; i < EXPORTS; i++) big += `export interface Model${i} { id: "${i}"; value: number }\nexport declare function get${i}(): Model${i};\n`;
fs.writeFileSync("src/models.ts", big);
for (let d = 0; d < DEPTH; d++) fs.writeFileSync(`src/barrel${d}.ts`, `export * from "${d === 0 ? "./models" : `./barrel${d - 1}`}";\nexport const barrel${d} = ${d};\n`);
for (let u = 0; u < USERS; u++) fs.writeFileSync(`src/user${u}.ts`, `import { get${u * 7 % EXPORTS} } from "./barrel${DEPTH - 1}";\nexport const v${u} = get${u * 7 % EXPORTS}();\n`);
fs.writeFileSync("tsconfig.json", JSON.stringify({ compilerOptions: { target: "es2022", module: "esnext", moduleResolution: "bundler", strict: true, noEmit: true, types: [], lib: ["es2022"] }, include: ["src"] }));
```

On an M3 Pro, the 207-file program above takes 0.23 s to check and makes 1.58M allocations. Nearly all of it is resolving the exports of the barrels.

For each module with `export *` declarations, `getExportsOfModuleWorker`:

- clones the module's own export table, and every nested module's table as it visits it;
- merges each re-exported table into a scratch table, allocating an `ExportCollision` struct (with the module specifier text) for every name, to be able to report TS2308 later;
- iterates that per-name table again looking for collisions, then merges the scratch table into the result;
- copies every visited module's export names into a set that is only consulted when an `export type *` was seen.

So each `export *` layer costs several allocations per re-exported name, even with a single `export *`, where nothing can collide.

In a private monorepo (programs of 8.6k and 9.6k files) whose barrels re-export large generated modules, the trackers alone were 5% of all allocations, and `extendExportSymbols` was the top source of map growth in the CPU profile.

Resolving a module's exports should cost about one map insert per name. Collision bookkeeping should only be needed when a module has more than one `export *`.

With the change in the linked PR, the generated program checks in 0.05 s with 0.24M allocations, and the monorepo programs check 3.6% and 4.7% faster. Diagnostics and symbol tables are unchanged.

The profiling and the patch were done with Claude Code.

Declaration emit renames value references when a shadowed type parameter shares its parameter's name

Searched open and closed issues in this repository and the legacy `microsoft/typescript-go` repository for: declaration emit, shadowed/rebound generic, same name type/value parameter, merged symbol, typeof, type predicate, asserts, generated parameter suffix, TS1225, and TS2693.

Related reports inspected: #16313, #47561 / #47609, typescript-go#3757 / typescript-go#3761, and #58456. I did not find a report of a merged type/value parameter symbol causing a generated type-parameter name to replace a predicate/assertion target or a `typeof` value operand. The examples below also demonstrate silent capture of a different parameter.

Reproduced with native TypeScript on macOS arm64:

- `7.1.0-dev.20261009.1` (`typescript@next` at the time of reporting), revision `6ad8c56f9b5a9bb910046c56059296311adc24ba`.
- `7.1.0-dev.20260929.1`, revision `0681ef7fa3a2378ccf49645b6d5b7463bdca74bb`.

I have not bisected older versions, so this report does not claim a regression boundary.

`src/repro.ts`:

```
export function predicate<value>(value: value) {
  return function <value>(value: value): value is value & string {
    return typeof value === "string";
  };
}

export function query<value>(value: value) {
  return function <value>(value: value): typeof value {
    return value;
  };
[... 1 more lines]
```

Compile with `strict: true`, `target: "ESNext"`, `module: "NodeNext"`, `declaration: true`, `emitDeclarationOnly: true`, `rootDir: "src"`, and `outDir: "dist"`. The source checks and declaration emit succeeds. Then check a consumer importing `dist/repro.js`, with `skipLibCheck: false`.

The emitted declarations are:

```
export declare function predicate<value>(value: value):
  <value_1>(value: value_1) => value_1 is value_1 & string;
export declare function query<value>(value: value):
  <value_1>(value: value_1) => typeof value_1;
```

The inner type parameter was renamed to `value_1`, but the value parameter declaration remains `value`. The emitter incorrectly changes references that need the **value** namespace:

- The predicate target produces TS1225: Cannot find parameter 'value_1'.
- The type-query operand produces TS2693: 'value_1' only refers to a type, but is being used as a value here.
- `asserts value` and `asserts value is value & string` have the same incorrect target rewrite.

It can also produce a legal declaration with the wrong meaning. Add a second parameter:

```
export function silentQuery<value>(value: value) {
  return function <value>(
    value: value,
    value_1: number
  ): typeof value {
    return value;
  };
}

export function silentPredicate<value>(value: value) {
[... 7 more lines]
```

Actual emit:

```
export declare function silentQuery<value>(value: value):
  <value_1>(value: value_1, value_1: number) => typeof value_1;
export declare function silentPredicate<value>(value: value):
  <value_1>(value: value_1, value_1: unknown) => value_1 is value_1 & string;
```

Both declarations check without declaration errors. The query now describes returning the second parameter's `number` type, although the implementation returns the first argument. The predicate now narrows the second argument, although the source predicate narrows the first. The same silent target change occurs with assertion predicates.

The generated type-parameter name should apply only to type references. For example:

```
export declare function predicate<value>(value: value):
  <value_1>(value: value_1) => value is value_1 & string;
export declare function query<value>(value: value):
  <value_1>(value: value_1) => typeof value;
```

Alternatively, a consistent rename of the value parameter declaration and all its value references would preserve the signature. The emitted API should retain the source's returned type and the predicate/assertion's parameter index.

- Distinct generic/value names pass, even when the inner generic still shadows the outer generic.
- Distinct outer and inner generic names avoid the issue.
- An explicit **outer return type** containing the nested callable avoids it.
- An explicit type annotation only on an inner callable variable, followed by an inferred outer return, still reproduces it.
- A fresh shared name for the entire inner type/value pair avoids it.
- Returned arrow functions, returned object methods, nested generic callback types, and deeper nested signatures also reproduce the problem.
- Type queries in inner parameter annotations also reproduce it: `return function <value>(value: value, other: typeof value) { return value; }` emits `other: typeof value_1`. A local annotated object type containing `typeof value`, returned by the inner function, can be corrupted too. An explicit inner return annotation is therefore not required for the type-query variant.
- Inferred non-nullish predicates preserve the value target in the tested nested case.

Potential cause at the September revision: [the shared identifier rewriter in nodecopy.go](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/0681ef7fa3a2378ccf49645b6d5b7463bdca74bb/tsc/internal/checker/nodecopy.go#L290-L307) uses `typeParameterToName` whenever a symbol has `TypeParameter` flags. Compatible type/value declarations can share that symbol. Both [type queries](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/0681ef7fa3a2378ccf49645b6d5b7463bdca74bb/tsc/internal/checker/nodecopy.go#L400-L407) and [explicit predicate targets](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/0681ef7fa3a2378ccf49645b6d5b7463bdca74bb/tsc/internal/checker/nodecopy.go#L762-L779) pass through that rewriter.

The reproductions were isolated with Codex and verified against both exact native compiler builds using separate source and emitted-declaration consumers. All source assertions passed; the corresponding declaration-consumer assertions expose the mismatches.

